            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p We can picture this as follows.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi e
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∃
                                %mi ψ
                                %mo ∈
                                %msup
                                  %mi p
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mn 1
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ∃
                            %mi e
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi E
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi p
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi e
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    e &amp;\stackrel{\exists \psi \in p^{-1}(\phi)}{\to} &amp; \exists e'&amp;&amp;&amp; E
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;  \downarrow^p
                    \\\
                    p(e) &amp;\stackrel{\phi}{\to} &amp; b &amp;&amp;&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              forgets can be “transported along isomorphisms.”
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              Notice that this definition of isofibration violates the 1-categorical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
              where it demands that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\psi)=\phi
              (which includes the requirement that
              = succeed "):" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(e') = b
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(\psi)\cong\phi
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%282%2C1%29-category"} (2,1)-category
              of categories/groupoids in terms of 1-categorical data. For more on this see below at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#AsFibrationsInCanonicalModelStructures"} As fibrations in canonical model structures
          %p
          %h2#section_Equivalent_definition Equivalent definition
          %p
            The following may at first seem a little surprising. It says that isofibrations have in fact a stronger lifting property, namely the analogue of that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+fibration"} Hurewicz fibration
            with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+object"} interval object
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Cat}
            given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+groupoid"} interval groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure"} model structure
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p: E \rightarrow B
              be a
              = succeed "\u00A0between" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
